ior woman  trying to maybe distract himself from  the fact that negan took his wife and  then another scene establishing gordon  at the farm maybe talking [ __ ] about  negan and asking Dwight how to escape  and then another couple scenes where  Dwight watches that red haired Savior  get jumped and then he loses bunk in  front of the guy's family now again this  could just be me reading into things and  maybe it was always written to be a  montage and it just didn't connect  clearly enough to the viewer or maybe  these scenes were just merged to save  time but considering AMC was pretty cool  with plenty of other episodes running  along the season my theory is that this  sequence was re-edited for total reason  either there was an extremely violent  moment from negan that the producers  didn't want so close after glennon  Abraham's death or maybe these scenes  were just too grim and dark and the  producers instead chose to open this  episode with a light-hearted fun sitcom  a music video  and I totally respect that choice like  this music and the montage were super  enjoyable and I just love seeing who's  the boss show up in the walking dead but  looking back this was a bit of a missed  opportunity episode 3 was the first we  saw of negan and his men following the  bloody premiere and really the show  could have used this moment who clearly  reinforced Megan's character and his  sick ideology and to retroactively Li  define Glenn and Abraham as casualties  and martyrs in a very violent war with a  very evil warlord instead the episode  open with this ironically lighthearted  montage that only vaguely hints at  negan's insanity while confusing us  about the Savior's social structure and  the unfortunate effect of all this is  we're left uncertain how to feel about  negan or whether Glenn and Abraham's  graphic deaths were really intended to  establish the savagery of a new villain  or were just for shock value now again I  don't think the Walking Dead is too  violent